Here, $n=6$
According to the question $\begin{array}{l}
\binom{6}{2} p^{2} q^{4}=4 \cdot\binom{6}{4} p^{4} q^{2} \\
\Rightarrow q^{2}=4 p^{2} \Rightarrow(1-p)^{2}=4 p^{2}
\end{array} \begin{array}{l}
\Rightarrow3 p^{2}+2 p-1=0 \\
\Rightarrow(p+1)(3 p-1)=0 \\
\Rightarrow p=\frac{1}{3} \quad(\because p \text{ cannot be negative })
\end{array}$
